Install it straight from GitHub; no sign-up or access request:

```bash
npm install github:bazingga08/strait-sdk-react-native#v0.8.0 @react-native-async-storage/async-storage react-native-play-install-referrer
```

Create the client once, in a file of its own:

```ts title="links.ts (@strait/sdk-react-native 0.8.0)"
import AsyncStorage from '@react-native-async-storage/async-storage';
import { PlayInstallReferrer } from 'react-native-play-install-referrer';
import { createStrait, fromPlayInstallReferrer } from '@strait/sdk-react-native';

export const links = createStrait({
  publishableKey: 'st_pub_live_…',        // Dashboard → Get started (safe in apps; never the secret key)
  endpoint: 'https://acme.strait.link',     // your Strait link host
  storage: AsyncStorage,            // required for reliable analytics
  installReferrer: fromPlayInstallReferrer(PlayInstallReferrer),
});

links.onLink((e) => console.log('Strait link:', e.matched, e.path, e.params));
links.start();
```

Import `./links` once from your app's entry file (`index.js` or `App.tsx`), then rebuild, because the SDK adds a native module.

**You should see:** the app starts normally. Nothing is logged yet.

Remove `com.google.firebase:firebase-dynamic-links` from `app/build.gradle.kts` and add the Strait SDK ([Install & set up](https://straitlink.in/docs/sdks/android/)). Then replace the listener:

```diff title="MainActivity.kt"
- Firebase.dynamicLinks.getDynamicLink(intent).addOnSuccessListener { data ->
-     data?.link?.let { openDeepLink(it.path, it.queryParameterNames.associateWith { k -> it.getQueryParameter(k)!! }) }
- }
+ links.onLink { e -> runOnUiThread { if (e.matched) openDeepLink(e.path, e.params) } }
+ if (savedInstanceState == null) links.start(intent?.dataString)
```

```kotlin title="MainActivity.kt"
override fun onNewIntent(intent: Intent) {
    super.onNewIntent(intent)
    intent.dataString?.let(links::handleUrl)   // replaces getDynamicLink(intent) for a running app
}
```

In the manifest, change the intent filter's host from `yourapp.page.link` to `acme.strait.link`, and add your custom scheme filter if you didn't have one ([manifest](https://straitlink.in/docs/sdks/android/#4-add-the-intent-filters)).

**You should see:** `adb shell am start -W -a android.intent.action.VIEW -d "https://acme.strait.link/summer"` opens the same screen the old Dynamic Link did.

```bash
npm uninstall @react-native-firebase/dynamic-links
npm install github:bazingga08/strait-sdk-react-native#v0.8.0 @react-native-async-storage/async-storage react-native-play-install-referrer
```

```diff title="App.tsx"
- import dynamicLinks from '@react-native-firebase/dynamic-links';
- dynamicLinks().getInitialLink().then((link) => link && handle(link.url));
- const unsubscribe = dynamicLinks().onLink((link) => handle(link.url));
+ import { links } from './links';   // createStrait({ publishableKey, endpoint, storage, installReferrer }); links.start();
+ const unsubscribe = links.onLink((e) => e.matched && e.path && openPath(e.path, e.params));
```

`links.ts` is on [Install & set up](https://straitlink.in/docs/sdks/react-native/#2-create-the-client-once). `openPath` maps a path to a screen ([Receive links](https://straitlink.in/docs/sdks/react-native/receive-links/)). Expo apps: [Expo (Expo Router)](https://straitlink.in/docs/sdks/expo/).

**You should see:** a tap on `https://acme.strait.link/summer` opens the screen, and so does the first open after installing from Google Play.

```bash
flutter pub remove firebase_dynamic_links
flutter pub add app_links shared_preferences play_install_referrer flutter_timezone
```

Add `strait_sdk` as a git dependency ([Install & set up](https://straitlink.in/docs/sdks/flutter/)), then:

```diff title="main.dart"
- final initial = await FirebaseDynamicLinks.instance.getInitialLink();
- if (initial != null) route(initial.link);
- FirebaseDynamicLinks.instance.onLink.listen((data) => route(data.link));
+ await startLinks();   // links.dart: StraitLinks(...) + links.start(...)
+ links.onLink.listen((e) { if (e.matched && e.path != null) routePath(e.path!, e.params ?? {}); });
```

**You should see:** the Flutter console logs `Strait link: direct app_link true /product/42` when you tap `https://acme.strait.link/summer`.

Replace the Firebase package with `StraitSDK` from `https://github.com/bazingga08/strait-sdk-swift`, and change Associated Domains from `applinks:yourapp.page.link` to `applinks:acme.strait.link`:

```diff title="SceneDelegate.swift"
  func scene(_ scene: UIScene, continue userActivity: NSUserActivity) {
-     guard let url = userActivity.webpageURL else { return }
-     DynamicLinks.dynamicLinks().handleUniversalLink(url) { link, _ in route(link?.url) }
+     links.handle(userActivity: userActivity)   // links.onLink { event in … } routes it
  }
```

The full setup, including the launch URL and SwiftUI, is on the [iOS page](https://straitlink.in/docs/sdks/ios/). iPhone support hasn't been proven on a real iPhone yet.

**You should see:** the app builds without `FirebaseDynamicLinks`, and `links.onLink` fires for a Universal Link on your domain.

**Unity.** Firebase's Unity plugin raised `DynamicLinks.DynamicLinkReceived`. Strait's plain C# library raises `OnLink`, and Unity's own `Application.deepLinkActivated` feeds it links while the game runs:

```diff title="LinksBehaviour.cs"
- DynamicLinks.DynamicLinkReceived += (sender, args) => Route(args.ReceivedDynamicLink.Url);
+ Client.OnLink += e => { if (e.Matched) Route(e.Path, e.Params); };
+ Application.deepLinkActivated += url => _ = Client.HandleUrl(url);
+ await Client.Start(Application.absoluteURL);   // launch link + deferred check
```

The full `LinksBehaviour` is on the [Unity page](https://straitlink.in/docs/sdks/unity/). Its engine glue hasn't been run in the Unity editor yet, so test it on a device before you ship.

**Web.** Dynamic Links sent desktop visitors to the `ofl` URL. Strait does the same with `desktopUrl`, or the destination itself, with no code. If your web app wants the link a visitor tapped on their phone before switching to the browser, the [web SDK](https://straitlink.in/docs/sdks/web/) asks once per browser.

**You should see:** a Unity build logs the link's path on a device, and a desktop browser opening `https://acme.strait.link/summer` lands on your website.

If your app built links with the Dynamic Links builder, move that to your backend, because creating links needs the secret key:

```diff title="ShareButton.tsx"
- const link = await dynamicLinks().buildShortLink({ link: productUrl, domainUriPrefix: 'https://yourapp.page.link' });
+ const { url: link } = await (await fetch('https://api.yourapp.com/share', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify({ productId }) })).json();
```

**You should see:** your share sheet offers `https://acme.strait.link/…` links. The server side is on [Create links in the app](https://straitlink.in/docs/sdks/react-native/create-links/).

For thousands of links, or to keep fallbacks and previews, send up to 500 per call to `POST /v1/links/batch`. Each item takes the same fields as `POST /v1/links`:

```js title="import.mjs (Node 18+)"
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';

const rows = readFileSync('links.csv', 'utf8').trim().split('\n').slice(1).map((l) => l.split(','));
const items = rows.map(([short, link]) => ({
  slug: new URL(short).pathname.slice(1),   // keep the old short code
  longUrl: link,
  campaign: 'fdl_import',
}));

for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i += 500) {
  const res = await fetch('https://strait.link/v1/links/batch', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.SECRET_KEY}`, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ links: items.slice(i, i + 500) }),
  });
  const { summary, results } = await res.json();
  console.log(summary, results.filter((r) => !r.ok));
}
```

Every item gets its own result, so one bad row never fails the batch. A slug that already exists is a `409` for that item only, so re-running the script is safe.

**You should see:** `{ total: 2, created: 2, failed: 0 }` for the example file. Failed items show their `status` and `error`.

### In the dashboard

Open **Dashboard → Import → From Branch**, choose the export file and select **Preview**. Each row shows the slug, the destination and the path your app will get, the fallbacks, the social preview, the campaign and tags, and any problems. Select **Import**. You get a summary of what was created and what wasn't (with the reason), and you can download the rows that failed as a CSV, fix them and import them again with **Bulk create**.

Imports are safe to run twice: a slug that already exists is reported, never overwritten.

### With the API

`POST /v1/links/batch` creates up to 500 links per call with your secret key. Each item takes the same fields as `POST /v1/links` and gets its own result, so one bad item never fails the batch. A slug that already exists (or repeats earlier in the batch) is a `409` for that item only.

```bash
curl https://strait.link/v1/links/batch \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $SECRET_KEY" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "links": [
      {
        "slug": "p42",
        "longUrl": "https://shop.example.com/product/42",
        "desktopUrl": "https://shop.example.com/products/42",
        "iosFallbackUrl": "https://shop.example.com/ios/42",
        "androidFallbackUrl": "https://shop.example.com/android/42",
        "title": "Product 42",
        "campaign": "summer_2026",
        "tags": ["sale"]
      },
      { "slug": "summer-sale", "longUrl": "https://shop.example.com/sale/summer?utm_source=instagram" }
    ]
  }'
```

```json
{
  "results": [
    { "index": 0, "ok": true, "status": 201, "link": { "slug": "p42", "shortUrl": "https://<handle>.strait.link/p42", … } },
    { "index": 1, "ok": false, "status": 409, "error": "slug already exists in this workspace" }
  ],
  "summary": { "total": 2, "created": 1, "failed": 1 }
}
```

Each item's `status` is what `POST /v1/links` would have answered: `201`, `400` (invalid, or `destination_blocked`), `402` (plan limit reached) or `409`. See the [REST API](https://straitlink.in/docs/api/#batch-create).

Remove the Branch SDK and add ours ([React Native](https://straitlink.in/docs/sdks/react-native/), [Flutter](https://straitlink.in/docs/sdks/flutter/), [Android](https://straitlink.in/docs/sdks/android/), [iOS](https://straitlink.in/docs/sdks/ios/), [Unity](https://straitlink.in/docs/sdks/unity/)). Where you read `$deeplink_path` from Branch's link data, route on `e.path` and `e.params` instead; the import put the deep link path there.

```diff
- branch.subscribe(({ params }) => { if (params['+clicked_branch_link']) route(params.$deeplink_path); });
+ links.onLink((e) => { if (e.matched && e.path) route(e.path, e.params); });
```

On Android and iOS that's the same change: drop `Branch.getAutoInstance()` and the `initSession` callback, start the Strait client at launch, and subscribe to `onLink`. Deferred links (the link someone tapped before installing) arrive through the same callback with `kind` set to `deferred`.

Events move from Branch events to `trackEvent`:

```diff
- new BranchEvent(BranchEvent.Purchase, null, { revenue: 49.99, currency: 'USD' }).logEvent();
+ links.trackEvent('purchase', { value: 49.99, currency: 'USD' });
```

Then update app verification to your link domain: on Android change the intent filter's `android:host` to `acme.strait.link` and add your SHA-256 fingerprints in Settings; on iPhone change Associated Domains to `applinks:acme.strait.link` and add your Team ID and bundle ID in Settings. See [App Links & Universal Links](https://straitlink.in/docs/app-links/).

Create it outside your components and start it when the app launches:

```ts title="links.ts (@strait/sdk-react-native 0.8.0)"
import AsyncStorage from '@react-native-async-storage/async-storage';
import { PlayInstallReferrer } from 'react-native-play-install-referrer';
import { createStrait, fromPlayInstallReferrer } from '@strait/sdk-react-native';

export const links = createStrait({
  publishableKey: 'st_pub_live_…',        // Dashboard → Get started (safe in apps; never the secret key)
  endpoint: 'https://acme.strait.link',     // your Strait link host
  storage: AsyncStorage,            // required for reliable analytics
  installReferrer: fromPlayInstallReferrer(PlayInstallReferrer),
});

links.start();
```

Import `./links` once from `index.js`. `endpoint` is your workspace's link domain: the SDK sends its requests there and treats links on that host as yours. Call `start()` once. `onLink` replays past events to late subscribers, so your navigator can subscribe after `start()` without missing the launch link.

**You should see:** the app starts as before after a rebuild (`npx react-native run-android`), because the SDK adds a native module.

Keep the mapping in one place, using a navigation ref so it works outside components:

```tsx title="navigation.ts"
import { createNavigationContainerRef } from '@react-navigation/native';

export const navigationRef = createNavigationContainerRef<any>();

// Map a link's path to one of your screens.
export function openPath(path: string, params: Record<string, string> = {}) {
  const product = /^\/product\/([^/]+)$/.exec(path);
  if (product) navigationRef.navigate('Product', { id: product[1], ...params });
  else navigationRef.navigate('Home');
}
```

**You should see:** the app compiles. Nothing calls `openPath` yet.

```tsx title="App.tsx"
import { NavigationContainer } from '@react-navigation/native';
import { links } from './links';
import { navigationRef, openPath } from './navigation';

export default function App() {
  return (
    <NavigationContainer
      ref={navigationRef}
      onReady={() => links.onLink((e) => e.matched && e.path && openPath(e.path, e.params))}
    >
      {/* your navigator */}
    </NavigationContainer>
  );
}
```

Don't also list your link domain in React Navigation's `linking` option, or each link is handled twice.

**You should see:** with the app closed, `adb shell am start -W -a android.intent.action.VIEW -d "yourapp://shop.example.com/product/42"` opens the Product screen with `id` `42`. Run it again with the app in the background: same screen.

A short link can take a second or more to resolve on a slow network. `onLinkStart` fires as soon as a link arrives:

```tsx title="LinkLoading.tsx"
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { ActivityIndicator, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';
import { links } from './links';

export function LinkLoading() {
  const [opening, setOpening] = useState(false);
  useEffect(() => {
    const offStart = links.onLinkStart(() => setOpening(true));
    const offLink = links.onLink(() => setOpening(false));
    return () => { offStart(); offLink(); };
  }, []);
  return opening ? <ActivityIndicator style={StyleSheet.absoluteFill} size="large" /> : null;
}
```

Render `<LinkLoading />` once, above your navigator.

**You should see:** a spinner for a moment when you tap a `https://acme.strait.link/…` link on a slow connection, then the screen.

With the [Node SDK](https://straitlink.in/docs/sdks/node/) (any language can call `POST /v1/links` instead):

```ts title="server/share.ts (Express)"
import { randomBytes } from 'node:crypto';
import express from 'express';
import { StraitClient } from '@strait/sdk-node';

const strait = new StraitClient({ apiKey: process.env.SECRET_KEY!, baseUrl: 'https://strait.link' });
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

// POST /share { "productId": "42" } → { "url": "https://acme.strait.link/…" }
app.post('/share', async (req, res) => {
  const id = String(req.body.productId).replace(/[^\w-]/g, '');
  const link = await strait.createLink({
    slug: `p${id}-${randomBytes(4).toString('hex')}`, // unique in your workspace
    longUrl: `https://shop.example.com/product/${id}?ref=${encodeURIComponent(req.user?.id ?? 'guest')}`,
    title: 'Look what I found',              // preview in WhatsApp and other chat apps
    campaign: 'in_app_share',
  });
  res.json({ url: link.shortUrl });
});
```

Slugs are unique within your workspace (letters, digits, `-` and `_`, up to 64), so add a random part. Check who's calling before you create links for them, as you would for any write.

**You should see:** `curl -X POST localhost:3000/share -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"productId":"42"}'` returns `{"url":"https://acme.strait.link/…"}`.

```tsx title="ShareButton.tsx"
import { Button, Share } from 'react-native';

export function ShareButton({ productId }: { productId: string }) {
  async function share() {
    const res = await fetch('https://api.yourapp.com/share', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },   // add your own auth header
      body: JSON.stringify({ productId }),
    });
    const { url } = await res.json();
    await Share.share({ message: url });
  }
  return <Button title="Share" onPress={share} />;
}
```

**You should see:** the system share sheet with a `https://acme.strait.link/…` link. Send it to yourself and tap it: the app opens on that product.

The destination's query arrives in `e.params`, on a direct open and on the first open after an install:

```ts title="links.ts"
links.onLink((e) => {
  if (e.matched && e.params?.ref) saveReferrer(e.params.ref); // your own code
});
```

**You should see:** `e.params.ref` set to the sharer's id when a friend opens the shared link, including after installing from the store.

If you only want the first-open-after-install destination, there's a one-call API. It never throws; on any error it returns `{ matched: false, matchMethod: 'none' }`.

```ts
import { resolveDeferredLink, splitUrl } from '@strait/sdk-react-native';
import { navigationRef, openPath } from './navigation';

const result = await resolveDeferredLink({
  publishableKey: 'st_pub_live_…',
  endpoint: 'https://acme.strait.link',
});
const dest = result.matched && result.longUrl ? splitUrl(result.longUrl) : null;
if (dest && navigationRef.isReady()) openPath(dest.path, dest.params);
```

Off by default. The SDK never touches the clipboard unless you set `clipboardBoost: true` **and** pass a clipboard adapter. Also turn on **Dashboard → Settings → Clipboard boost**.

```ts
import * as Clipboard from 'expo-clipboard';
import { createStrait, fromExpoClipboard } from '@strait/sdk-react-native';

export const links = createStrait({
  publishableKey: 'st_pub_live_…',
  endpoint: 'https://acme.strait.link',
  clipboardBoost: true,
  clipboard: fromExpoClipboard(Clipboard),
});
```

On the first launch only, the SDK asks whether the clipboard probably holds a web link (iOS answers without a prompt). Only if it does, it reads the text, and iOS shows its **Allow Paste** prompt. Anything that isn't a Strait handoff link never leaves the phone.

Replace `acme.strait.link` with your own link domain, and `yourapp` with the custom scheme in Dashboard → Settings:

```json title="app.json"
{
  "expo": {
    "scheme": "yourapp",
    "android": {
      "package": "com.yourcompany.yourapp",
      "intentFilters": [
        {
          "action": "VIEW",
          "autoVerify": true,
          "data": [{ "scheme": "https", "host": "acme.strait.link" }],
          "category": ["BROWSABLE", "DEFAULT"]
        }
      ]
    },
    "ios": {
      "bundleIdentifier": "com.yourcompany.yourapp",
      "associatedDomains": ["applinks:acme.strait.link"]
    }
  }
}
```

`scheme` registers `yourapp://` on both platforms (used when a browser hands a link to your app). The intent filter with `autoVerify` and `associatedDomains` let `https://acme.strait.link/…` links open the app directly once your fingerprints and Team ID are in Settings.

**You should see:** `npx expo config --type public` prints your `scheme`, `intentFilters` and `associatedDomains`.

Create the client (the same `links.ts` as [bare React Native](https://straitlink.in/docs/sdks/react-native/#2-create-the-client-once)), then subscribe in your root layout once navigation is ready:

```tsx title="app/_layout.tsx"
import { Stack, router, useRootNavigationState, type Href } from 'expo-router';
import { useEffect } from 'react';
import { links } from '../links';

export default function RootLayout() {
  const ready = useRootNavigationState()?.key;
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!ready) return;
    // Opened from a link, or first open after install: same callback.
    return links.onLink((e) => {
      if (e.matched && e.path) router.push({ pathname: e.path, params: e.params } as Href);
    });
  }, [ready]);
  return <Stack />;
}
```

Expo Router also tries to open every incoming link itself, and `https://acme.strait.link/summer` isn't a screen in your app. This file sends it to your first screen and lets `onLink` do the routing:

```tsx title="app/+native-intent.tsx"
// Links reach the app through Strait's onLink (app/_layout.tsx), which knows the
// real destination. Keep Expo Router from routing the raw link URL.
export function redirectSystemPath({ path }: { path: string; initial: boolean }) { return '/'; }
```

If your app has other deep links of its own (a sign-in callback, for example), return `path` for those instead of `'/'`.

**You should see:** no "Unmatched route" screen when a link opens the app.

```dart title="links.dart"
import 'dart:async';
import 'dart:io' show Platform;
import 'dart:ui';

import 'package:app_links/app_links.dart';
import 'package:strait_sdk/strait_sdk.dart';
import 'package:flutter/widgets.dart';
import 'package:flutter_timezone/flutter_timezone.dart';
import 'package:play_install_referrer/play_install_referrer.dart';
import 'package:shared_preferences/shared_preferences.dart';

/// Persists the once-per-install flag and unsent open reports.
class PrefsStore implements KeyValueStore {
  PrefsStore(this.prefs);
  final SharedPreferences prefs;
  @override
  Future<String?> get(String key) async => prefs.getString(key);
  @override
  Future<void> set(String key, String value) => prefs.setString(key, value);
}

/// Feeds Flutter lifecycle changes to the SDK.
class LifecycleFeed with WidgetsBindingObserver {
  final _ctl = StreamController<AppLifecycle>.broadcast(sync: true);
  Stream<AppLifecycle> get stream => _ctl.stream;
  LifecycleFeed() { WidgetsBinding.instance.addObserver(this); }
  @override
  void didChangeAppLifecycleState(AppLifecycleState s) => _ctl.add(switch (s) {
        AppLifecycleState.resumed => AppLifecycle.active,
        AppLifecycleState.inactive => AppLifecycle.inactive,
        _ => AppLifecycle.background,
      });
}

late final StraitLinks links;

Future<void> startLinks() async {
  WidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();
  final timezone = await FlutterTimezone.getLocalTimezone(); // IANA, e.g. Asia/Kolkata
  links = StraitLinks(
    publishableKey: 'st_pub_live_…',      // Dashboard → Get started
    endpoint: 'https://acme.strait.link',   // your workspace's link domain
    platform: Platform.isIOS ? 'ios' : Platform.isAndroid ? 'android' : 'other',
    storage: PrefsStore(await SharedPreferences.getInstance()),
    installReferrer: () async =>
        Platform.isAndroid ? (await PlayInstallReferrer.installReferrer).installReferrer : null,
    deviceFields: () {
      final view = PlatformDispatcher.instance.views.first;
      return DeviceFields(
        screenWidth: portraitScreenWidth(view.physicalSize.width / view.devicePixelRatio, view.physicalSize.height / view.devicePixelRatio),
        pixelRatio: view.devicePixelRatio,
        language: PlatformDispatcher.instance.locale.toLanguageTag(),
        timezone: timezone,
      );
    },
  );
  links.onLink.listen((e) => debugPrint('Strait link: ${e.kind.name} ${e.route.value} ${e.matched} ${e.path}'));

  final appLinks = AppLinks();
  await links.start(
    initialUrl: (await appLinks.getInitialLink())?.toString(),
    urls: appLinks.uriLinkStream.map((u) => u.toString()),
    lifecycle: LifecycleFeed().stream,
  );
}
```

Call `await startLinks();` in `main()` before `runApp`. Time zone: pass the IANA name (`Asia/Kolkata`); `DateTime.timeZoneName` is an abbreviation on some platforms and won't match. With `flutter_timezone` 4.x use `.identifier`. `app_links` 6+ also emits the launch link on `uriLinkStream`; `start()` ignores that first echo.

**You should see:** the app starts as before.

If your app uses [`go_router`](https://pub.dev/packages/go_router), subscribe once the router exists:

```dart title="main.dart"
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:go_router/go_router.dart';
import 'links.dart';

final router = GoRouter(routes: [
  GoRoute(path: '/', builder: (_, __) => const HomeScreen()),
  GoRoute(path: '/product/:id', builder: (_, s) => ProductScreen(id: s.pathParameters['id']!)),
]);

Future<void> main() async {
  await startLinks();
  links.onLink.listen((e) {
    if (e.matched && e.path != null) {
      router.go(Uri(path: e.path, queryParameters: e.params).toString());
    }
  });
  runApp(MaterialApp.router(routerConfig: router));
}
```

**You should see:** with the app closed, `adb shell am start -W -a android.intent.action.VIEW -d "yourapp://shop.example.com/product/42"` opens `ProductScreen` with `id` `42`.

Without a router package, use a global key so the listener can navigate from anywhere:

```dart title="main.dart"
final navigatorKey = GlobalKey<NavigatorState>();

void openPath(String path, Map<String, String> params) {
  final product = RegExp(r'^/product/([^/]+)$').firstMatch(path);
  if (product != null) {
    navigatorKey.currentState?.push(MaterialPageRoute(builder: (_) => ProductScreen(id: product.group(1)!)));
  }
}

